<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"className":"ss-h2","align":"center"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-align-center ss-h2">For municipalities</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-cards sp-stagger","align":"wide","layout":{"type":"constrained","contentSize":"1200px","wideSize":"1200px"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignwide ss-cards sp-stagger"><!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up","layout":{"type":"default"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__name"} -->
<p class="ss-card__name">Water rounds for councils and parishes</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__body"} -->
<p class="ss-card__body">A council or a parish uses the same water fees as any other client. A sampling round is $2,250 and covers up to 6 points, with laboratory results in 15 working days. A year of returns is the annual programme: 4 rounds at $6,600 per site. The parish council at Hollin took that path in 2024 for 5 village wells, filed as GW-07. Five wells sit inside the six-point round. The report states the results against the code and stops at those results. It does not declare the water safe to drink. The way in is the free scoping call, 20 minutes, held within 2 working days, on +1 555 0142 300 or by email.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up","layout":{"type":"default"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__name"} -->
<p class="ss-card__name">A habitat baseline on council land</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__body"} -->
<p class="ss-card__body">Where the municipal question is a meadow, a reed-bed or a restored bank, the instruction is the habitat and biodiversity survey at $4,200. Fieldwork runs from April to September. In 2021 Alder Bridge council instructed a floodplain meadow baseline at the Wend Meadow reach, site WM-03, with 3-year monitoring. The baseline describes the habitat on those visits. Later monitoring describes it on the return visits. We do not attach a recovery score, because no score of that kind is on the fee list. The written report and the 45-minute review are the handover. A monitoring year of 4 rounds, if it is wanted as well, is $6,600 for the site and is instructed on its own row.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up","layout":{"type":"default"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__name"} -->
<p class="ss-card__name">Liaison while the file is public</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__body"} -->
<p class="ss-card__body">Permitting and municipal liaison is Priya Raman's role. It is not a seventh fee. It is how a priced instruction is handled when the reader will not only be the project team: one scope, the published USD amount, 50% on instruction and 50% when the report is issued, and a clear line between our findings and the authority's own decision. She will not predict a permit or a public reaction. Use this when the file is a parish well programme or a council meadow. Written correspondence goes to 14 Millrace Lane, Alder Bridge, or to hello@seisui-environmental.example. Hours for that correspondence are Mon–Fri 08:30–17:30, with field days by arrangement.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div></div>
