<p class="ss-ghost has-text-align-center" aria-hidden="true">FOR LAND</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"className":"ss-h2","align":"center"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-align-center ss-h2">For landowners</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-cards sp-stagger","align":"wide","layout":{"type":"constrained","contentSize":"1200px","wideSize":"1200px"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group alignwide ss-cards sp-stagger"><!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up","layout":{"type":"default"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__name"} -->
<p class="ss-card__name">A first walk of the land</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__body"} -->
<p class="ss-card__body">A landowner who wants a first reading, before any drilling, instructs a site walkover and desk study for $1,850. We complete it within 10 working days. It suits a field margin, a river bank or a small lot: the records first, then a walk of what is there. You are welcome on the walk. The note that comes back names a next step only when one is justified, whether that is Phase I, a water round, a habitat survey, or no further work. Afterwards we can take the 45-minute review at the office, 14 Millrace Lane, in hours Mon–Fri 08:30–17:30. Field days sit outside the counter hours and are arranged. The walkover fee is the full price of that step.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up","layout":{"type":"default"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__name"} -->
<p class="ss-card__name">Phase I and Phase II before a resale</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__body"} -->
<p class="ss-card__body">Before a resale, the pair we actually use is Phase I at $3,400 and, where Phase I shows the soil should be opened, Phase II from $8,900. In 2023 a private landowner used both on the former tannery lot at Fennick Road, now SL-02. Phase I did not drill. Phase II investigated the soil under a scope agreed in advance. The written report and a 45-minute review closed the instruction, with 50% paid on instruction and 50% on the report. The outcome we will state is that the phases were completed and reported before resale. We will not state that the lot was clear of its industrial past, or that a buyer is protected by having our report in the pack.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up","layout":{"type":"default"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ss-card ss-card--text sp-reveal sp-reveal--up"><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__name"} -->
<p class="ss-card__name">Habitat where the question is the vegetation</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"ss-card__body"} -->
<p class="ss-card__body">When the land question is vegetation rather than contamination, the fee is the habitat and biodiversity survey at $4,200, carried out from April to September. The three parts, under that one fee, are a habitat walk, a quadrat on the grassland, and a written baseline. A landowner who then wants four returns in a year can add the annual programme at $6,600 for the site. That is the structure behind WM-03, Wend Meadow, which was commissioned as a baseline with 3-year monitoring rather than as a single unrepeated visit. The survey says what was growing on the day. A new planting is described if it is present. The fee does not promise that the planting will take.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div>
